  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Miles Davis entered the documented maritime-preservation record in 1995, when he rescued a historic American steel motor vessel from a Miami scrapyard. Moving the craft to Northern California, he sustained it for thirteen years across San Francisco Bay and the Sacramento–San Joaquin Delta. Preservation histories credit that intervention with preventing the loss of an important surviving example of prewar American design.\n\nHis work also brought a leadership role in the Classic Yacht Association’s Northern California fleet, where he served as commodore in 2008. Contemporary association reporting placed his stewardship within a wider effort to maintain ageing historic craft despite rising restoration and operating costs. The vessel later passed to another preservation-minded owner for a comprehensive restoration, extending the legacy Davis had protected.",
    "html": "<p>Miles Davis entered the documented maritime-preservation record in 1995, when he rescued a historic American steel motor vessel from a Miami scrapyard. Moving the craft to Northern California, he sustained it for thirteen years across San Francisco Bay and the Sacramento–San Joaquin Delta. Preservation histories credit that intervention with preventing the loss of an important surviving example of prewar American design.</p>\r\n<p>His work also brought a leadership role in the Classic Yacht Association’s Northern California fleet, where he served as commodore in 2008. Contemporary association reporting placed his stewardship within a wider effort to maintain ageing historic craft despite rising restoration and operating costs. The vessel later passed to another preservation-minded owner for a comprehensive restoration, extending the legacy Davis had protected.</p>\r\n",
